<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ulric Manfred II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 992–<a href="/years/1034">1034</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Olderico Manfredi II · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arduinici">Arduinici</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ulric Manfred II was margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> from about 1000 until his death in 1034, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arduinici">Arduinici</a> family. He controlled the Alpine routes over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-cenis">Mont Cenis</a> and Mont Genèvre passes, extended the march across much of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piedmont">Piedmont</a> and founded monasteries, including San Giusto at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a>. His daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adelaide-of-susa">Adelaide of Susa</a> inherited his lands and, through her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-count-of-savoy">Otto of Savoy</a>, brought them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, Ulric Manfred, also called Manfred Ulric, was the son of Manfred I and of Prangarda, a daughter of Adalbert Atto of Canossa. He inherited a large march centred on Turin, formed from the lands of his ancestor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arduin-glaber">Arduin Glaber</a>, and was the last male margrave of the Arduinid dynasty. An imperial diploma of 31 July 1001 records that Emperor Otto III confirmed his possessions and granted him privileges for his faithful service. By 1014 at the latest he had married Bertha, with whom he had three daughters, Adelaide, Immilla and Bertha.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From his succession he strengthened his position against Arduin of the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivrea">Ivrea</a> and against Henry II, gaining much territory from Ivrea in the struggle for the kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> while avoiding open conflict with either leader. Charters of 1021 and 1031 show him controlling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, Ivrea, Albenga, Ventimiglia, Auriate, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tortona">Tortona</a> and Vercelli. He was at war with Boniface III, margrave of Tuscany, in 1016, and after Henry II died in 1024 he opposed the election of Conrad II and invited William V of Aquitaine to take the Italian throne, without success.</p><p>Though he had a palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, he moved from castle to castle to govern his lands, and he fortified the villages of Exilles and Bardonecchia. Around 1028 he joined his brother, Bishop Alric of Asti, Archbishop Aribert of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and Bishop Landulf of Turin in suppressing a heretical movement at Monforte.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ulric Manfred restored the church of Santa Maria Maggiore in Susa and Novalesa Abbey, and with Bertha founded the convent of Santa Maria at Caramagna in May 1028. In July 1029, with Bertha and Bishop Alric, he founded the Benedictine abbey of San Giusto in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> to house the relics of Saint Justus of Novalesa; its church is now Susa Cathedral. He was buried in Turin Cathedral. Through Adelaide's marriage around 1045 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odo-count-of-savoy">Otto of Savoy</a>, a younger son of Count Humbert I of Savoy, the march passed to the line that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a>, later rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ulric Manfred II of Turin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ulric Manfred II</b> (died 1034) extended the march and founded monasteries.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-turin">March of Turin</a></li></ul></section>
